Without being definite about this matter, let us examine what Jesus said. To whom was Jesus talking when he made that statement about the resurrection?
Why have our publications said that Jesus was probably talking about a resurrection on earth? There are mainly two reasons.
However, it seems possible that Jesus was referring to a resurrection to heaven. What reasons do we have for saying this?
Let us examine two statements in Jesus ’ reply to the Sadducees.
We learn that Jesus may have been referring to a resurrection to heaven. If so, then we understand three things about those who are resurrected to heaven: (1) They do not marry, (2) they cannot die, and (3) in some ways they are similar to angels in heaven.
But if Jesus was referring to a resurrection to heaven, the following questions need to be answered. First, the Sadducees were probably thinking about a resurrection on earth, so why would Jesus refer to a resurrection to heaven?
Before mentioning these faithful men, Jesus began his statement with the words “regarding the resurrection of the dead. ” This phrase may allow for a change in the discussion from a resurrection to heaven to a resurrection on earth.
Jesus wanted to prove to the Sadducees that a resurrection on earth is part of God’s purpose. — Exodus 3: 1 - 6. Third, if Jesus ’ words apply to the resurrection to heaven, does this mean that those who are resurrected on earth will be able to marry?
God’s Word does not give a clear answer to that question. If Jesus was talking about the resurrection to heaven, then his words do not explain whether resurrected ones on earth will be able to marry in the new world.
But one thing is certain: Those who obey God will be happy, because Jehovah will satisfy all their needs and desires in the best way. — Psalm 145: 16.
