What does a teacher need to create, and why?
First, a teacher needs to create the right conditions. Why is this important?
Just as a gardener needs to prepare the soil before he plants seeds, a teacher needs to prepare a learner’s heart before he can teach him new skills.
So how can a teacher create the right conditions for training? He can follow the example of a skillful teacher of the past, the prophet Samuel.
When they arrived at Samuel’s house, they went up to the roof, and Samuel “continued to speak with Saul on the housetop ” until they went to sleep. The next day, Samuel anointed Saul, kissed him, and gave him more instructions.
Teachers, take time out of your busy schedule to be with the learner How do elders today imitate Samuel, and what joy does that give them?
Instead, they feel that these brothers are precious “fellow workers ” who will help them care for the needs of the congregation.
How can an elder prepare the heart of a learner for training, and why is that important?
It is true that a successful teacher loves to train others. But a successful teacher must also love the person he is training.
Because if a learner feels that you truly care about him, he will be willing to learn from you. So elders, make an effort not only to teach but also to be a good friend. — Proverbs 17: 17; John 15: 15.
These elders live in Australia, Bangladesh, Belgium, Brazil, France, French Guiana, Japan, Korea, Mexico, Namibia, Nigeria, Réunion, Russia, South Africa, and the United States.
BROTHERS and sisters from all over the world have moved to congregations in countries where the need for publishers is greater. They work hard to preach and teach the good news.
In many places, there would not be enough trained local brothers to care for the needs of the congregation.
To prepare a learner’s heart: To help a brother to be willing to learn new skills. A teacher does this by taking the time to become the learner’s friend.
How can those who are being trained imitate Elisha?
GOD’S people have always known that training brings success.
What methods have some elders used to train brothers to care for Jehovah’s people?
Ask a learner what his goals are in Jehovah’s service.
Explain how his work will make the Kingdom message more attractive.
Also, ask the learner to think about the older ones in the congregation and how doing this work will help them. A conversation such as this provides training that will help him to think more about people rather than rules.
Try to touch his heart by asking questions such as: ‘ What did you promise Jehovah when you dedicated your life to him?
Such scriptures can be very powerful and can deeply affect a brother’s heart. — Read Hebrews 4: 12.
Young brothers, the congregation needs your help!
What attitude will help you to have success in your service to Jehovah?
To find the answer, let us discuss some events in the life of a learner in the past, Elisha. About 3,000 years ago, the prophet Elijah invited young Elisha to become his helper.
Be quick to accept assignments, even humble tasks. Remember that your teacher is your friend.
When you prove that you are faithful and reliable, the elders can be confident that Jehovah wants you to have more responsibilities in the congregation. — Psalm 101: 6; read 2 Timothy 2: 2.
Did you notice that Elisha’s first miracle was exactly the same as Elijah’s last miracle?
What does this teach us?
As you gain more experience, you will apply changes that can help the congregation to follow Jehovah’s organization, which is always moving ahead.
ELDERS who are successful in training others give these suggestions:
Be an example in what you say and in what you do.
Encourage the learner to strengthen his relationship with Jehovah by reading the entire Bible in one year.
Work together in field service.
Teach him to conduct meetings for field service that benefit the brothers.
When you give a public talk, give him the outline so that he can see how you developed the subject.
At times, invite him and his family to join you for your family worship.
Ask him and his family to go with you and your family to preach in areas where there are few or no Witnesses.
Experienced elders train brothers by teaching them important skills that they will need in order to care for God’s people
As told by Trophim R. Nsomba
Despite the persecution and all the difficulties, the number of congregations increased in the circuits where we served. One day, we came to a village where there was a political meeting going on.
Suddenly, an older woman came running and yelled: “Leave them alone, please!
Let him go on his journey! ” The person in charge of the meeting said: “Let them go! ”
We do not know why the woman said what she did, because she was not our relative. We believe that Jehovah must have heard our prayer.
He removed the current pastor, telling him: “I don’t want you to teach God’s Word here again, for you were put in prison because you stole from your church! ”
In October 1984, all of us went to court and were sentenced to two years in prison. As before, we were put in a prison with non - Witnesses.
So we carried him back to the prison. Because we continued to have good conduct, we were able to see Jehovah’s name praised by the men who guarded us. — 1 Peter 2: 12.
In fact, we felt that we had received the sentence of death. This was so that we would trust, not in ourselves, but in the God who raises up the dead.
But we always asked Jehovah to give us courage, wisdom, and humility so that we could continue to bring glory to his great name. Jehovah has blessed our service to him during “favorable times and difficult times. ”
Brothers who have young children at home are no longer invited to serve in the circuit work.
For more information about the persecution in Malawi, see the 1999 Yearbook of Jehovah’s Witnesses, pages 171 - 223.
During the preparation of this article, Brother Nsomba died at the age of 83.
What should your goal be when you study the Bible?
How do you know that Jehovah always does what is right?
Why should you ask for specific things in your prayers?
You have a personal relationship with Jehovah.
Is Jehovah real to you?
Do you feel that he is your Friend?
Would you like your relationship with him to be even stronger?
But what are some things you can do to draw closer to Jehovah? How can you communicate with God?
If you want to draw close to Jehovah, it is very important that you and Jehovah communicate regularly.
Well, think about how you and a friend who lives far away might communicate.
How does Jehovah speak to you when you study the Bible?
For example, how do you feel when you read Jesus ’ words: “Stop storing up for yourselves treasures on the earth ”? If you are doing your best to put Jehovah first in your life, you will feel that he is pleased with you.
What details help us to understand why Jehovah punished Azariah with leprosy? The Bible does give us more details about the life of King Azariah, who was also known as King Uzziah.
But it also says that later “his heart became haughty to his own ruin. ”
The king tried to do something that only priests were allowed to do. Eighty - one priests told him that he was wrong and tried to stop him.
Why do we not need to know the reasons for everything Jehovah does?
How can we have more trust that Jehovah always does what is right?
What important lesson do we learn?
In the story of King Azariah, we have enough details to help us understand why Jehovah punished him.
Will you wonder if Jehovah really did what was right?
Or will you feel that the Bible has enough information for you to trust that God always does what is right? The more you get to know Jehovah as a Person, the more you will love and trust him.
How do you know that Jehovah listens to prayers?
Some people say that God does not listen to prayers and that saying a prayer is just something that makes you feel better. They believe that prayer simply helps you to think carefully about your problems and to find your own answers.
How can you be sure? You can be sure that Jehovah listens to your prayers
Think about this: Before Jesus came to earth, he saw that Jehovah answered the prayers of His human servants. When on earth, Jesus prayed to his Father in heaven about his thoughts and feelings.
Jesus also taught his followers how to pray to Jehovah. Would he have done that if he thought that Jehovah does not listen to prayers?
So let us keep listening to him by regularly studying the Bible, and let us keep speaking to him by praying. If we do this, our relationship with Jehovah will become stronger and stronger, and with his help we will be able to endure trials.
Jehovah is real to you when you know him as a Person and you have a close friendship with him. You know that he speaks to you by means of his Word, the Bible, and you know that you can trust him.
You communicate with Jehovah when you speak to him in prayer, and Jehovah communicates with you when you read and study the Bible
Why does Jehovah not always answer prayers right away?
How does Jehovah’s promise at 1 Corinthians 10: 13 comfort you?
What work will help you to build trust in Jehovah and make your relationship with him stronger?
Why was Paul convinced that he could trust in Jehovah?
We can read about many examples in the Bible that convince us that Jehovah really does help his people. A trial gives us an opportunity to trust in Jehovah completely
Why might it not be easy to trust in Jehovah when we have a difficult problem?
Jehovah, our Father in heaven, loves us and knows what we need Why does Jehovah not always answer our prayers right away?
The Bible says that Jehovah is our Father and we are his children.
He will help you to endure and be faithful to him. — Psalm 32: 8. Trust that your heavenly Father will give you the strength to obey him faithfully
How did trusting in Jehovah help Rhonda endure one tragedy after another? Our complete trust in Jehovah will even help us to endure tragedies.
It taught me to rely on him rather than on myself or even on other people.
You know what the Bible says about how to treat disfellowshipped ones.
Did he believe that he could disobey Jehovah and keep on living?
Adam ate the fruit that his wife gave him because he loved his wife more than he loved Jehovah. He listened to his wife instead of listening to Jehovah’s command. — Genesis 3: 6, 17.
So keep strengthening your love for Jehovah and your trust in him.
Show Jehovah that you trust in him by keeping busy in the preaching work (See paragraph 17) If we keep busy in the preaching work, what can we trust that Jehovah will do?
How can you build trust in Jehovah and make your relationship with him stronger?
We trust in Jehovah by praying to him and by patiently waiting for him to answer our prayers. In the meantime, we keep busy serving him, knowing that he will give us the strength to endure
What reasons does the Bible give for disfellowshipping someone?
And since it causes so much pain, why can we say that disfellowshipping is an act of love?
If one of Jehovah’s Witnesses who is baptized commits a serious sin and does not repent, he will be disfellowshipped.
Jehovah does not expect us to be perfect, but he does expect his servants to obey his laws and to remain holy. For example, Jehovah commands his servants to avoid serious sins such as sexual immorality, idolatry, stealing, extortion, murder, and spiritism. — 1 Corinthians 6: 9, 10; Revelation 21: 8.
What Jehovah expects of us is reasonable and is a protection for us. For example, we all want to live among peaceful, decent, and honest people, and that is what we experience among our spiritual brothers and sisters.
Because they dedicated themselves to Jehovah and promised to live by what he says in the Bible. But what if a baptized Christian commits a serious sin because of human weakness?
This happened to some of Jehovah’s servants in the past, but Jehovah did not reject them completely.
For example, even though King David committed adultery and murder, the prophet Nathan told David that Jehovah had forgiven his sin. — 2 Samuel 12: 13.
Today, too, someone who sinned will be forgiven if he is truly repentant and stops doing what is bad. If the elders on a judicial committee do not discern that the person is truly repentant, then he must be disfellowshipped.
If someone in your family or a close friend has been disfellowshipped, you may find it hard to accept that decision and may feel that it is too harsh.
However, Jehovah’s Word clearly shows us that disfellowshipping is an act of love. Jesus said that “wisdom is proved righteous by its results. ”
When someone is disfellowshipped, he is no longer a part of that family. After experiencing the results of his bad conduct, he may realize his mistake and remember how much happier he was when he had a good relationship with Jehovah and the congregation.
It is important that all of us allow the discipline to work. How can we do that?
Family members show love for the congregation and the disfellowshipped person when they support the decision made by the elders. Julian explains, “He was still my son, but his lifestyle had put up a barrier between us. ”
We should never make them feel that they have also been excluded from the congregation. — Romans 12: 13, 15. “ Disfellowshipping is an arrangement that we need, one that helps us live according to Jehovah’s standards, ” Julian concludes.
Just as a farmer is eager to see an olive tree that was cut down come back to life, Jehovah God is eager to resurrect his servants and many others. Imagine how joyful we will be as we welcome back the dead and see them alive once again!
