When the Theocratic Ministry School began in 1943, I started giving student talks in my congregation. The counsel I received after my parts on the school helped me to become a better speaker.
I spent the first two weeks in a prison in Columbus, Ohio, and I stayed in my cell the first day. I prayed to Jehovah: “I cannot stay in a cell for five years.
I walked over to a tall, broad - shouldered prisoner, and we stood there looking out a window.
So while I was there, I had no problems with the 50 prisoners in that section. I was among the Witnesses imprisoned for neutrality at Ashland, Kentucky
Seven in my family were attending meetings and one of my sisters was baptized, despite Dad’s opposition. Going out in the ministry with Demetrius Papageorge, an anointed brother who began serving Jehovah in 1913
Soon afterward, I attended the Bethel meeting at a convention in Cincinnati, Ohio. Brother Milton Henschel told us that there was a need at Bethel for brothers who wanted to work hard for the Kingdom.
For several years, I operated the boilers in the printery and the office buildings, worked as a machinist, and repaired locks. I also worked at Assembly Halls in New York City.
Now I have a hard time preaching from door to door because of my age, but I have not given up. I have learned a little Mandarin Chinese and enjoy approaching Chinese people on the street.
