As told by Samuel F. Herd
I was born in a one - room log cabin in a very small town called Liberty, Indiana, U.S.A. My parents already had one son and two daughters when I was born.
The log cabin where I was born
When I was born, my father worked for a local farmer. As a teenager, I learned to drive a tractor and to do other farmwork.
Why? I had decided to get married to Gloria, one of those two young ladies who had invited me to the book study!
Because we did not have our mom anymore, my dad made an arrangement with the school principal. My older sister was then in her last year of high school, and Dad asked if she and I could go to school on alternate days.
If black Witnesses preached from door to door in a white neighborhood, they would be arrested and probably beaten up. So in order to get the preaching work done, we obeyed the laws and hoped that things would change for the better.
That is how things were in those days.
On the other hand, we had wonderful times with the brothers, and we loved our ministry! When we first arrived in Pine Bluff, we lived with the brother who was the congregation servant at the time.
One Halloween night I remember seeing a man sitting on his front porch, proudly wearing a white sheet and hood, as those in the KKK did.
They finally told me that they wanted to give me the job. What would I do?
I had made my decision. I am so glad that I did not take that job!
Here is how Gloria remembers our time in Pine Bluff: “I fell in love with the territory!
So we would go in the house - to - house work in the morning and then conduct Bible studies the rest of the day, sometimes until 11 o’clock at night. Service was so much fun!
I have to admit that I did not really want to change my assignment and enter the circuit work, but Jehovah had something else in mind. ”
While we were pioneering in Pine Bluff, we applied to become special pioneers. We really expected to be appointed.
Brother Leon Weaver, now the coordinator of the United States Branch Committee, was appointed to serve as a circuit overseer at the same time. I was nervous about becoming a circuit overseer.
I learned a lot from that faithful brother. I cherish the help I received from experienced brothers
I thought that the responsible brothers at Bethel only wanted me to help out for a short time, but that is not what happened. Gloria was a jewel when I married her, and she still is
My first assignment was in the Service Department. I learned so much!
